00:00:37.175 --> 00:00:38.550
CARTER MORGAN:
Yeah, I went back,

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.150
I rewatched all
the old episodes.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.070
We talked about nodes.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.810
We talked about
containers, and we even

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.420
talked about the basic
Kubernetes API objects.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.060
But one thing was we deployed
everything imperatively,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.695
which you said wasn't
going to scale well, right?

00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:51.570
KASLIN FIELDS: Right.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.520
So this time I'm
going to show you how

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.485
to deploy an app declaratively.

00:00:56.485 --> 00:00:57.360
CARTER MORGAN: Great.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.262
I can't wait to
see how it works.

00:01:00.262 --> 00:01:01.970
KASLIN FIELDS: Now
we're going to explore

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.630
how to deploy applications in
containers declaratively using

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:06.800
Kubernetes.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.460
If you remember
in the last video,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.580
we talked about
deployments in Kubernetes

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.410
and how to create them.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.560
But the command that we
used in our last video

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.500
to create a deployment
was imperative.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.230
We told Kubernetes exactly
what we wanted it to do

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.460
and it did it.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.650
In the real world,
we don't really

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.510
want to do things that way.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:29.090
We want to do
things declaratively

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.720
by having a file that
describes or declares

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.490
what we want things to be like.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.040
And then we have Kubernetes do
the hard work of figuring out

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.260
how to make that happen.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:43.370
Having our state defined
declaratively in files

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.760
allows us to do some cool
things, like make changes

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.950
to our deployments
by just changing

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.480
the file describing it.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.720
We can also track those
changes through source control

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.480
and make use of great tools
for deploying applications

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.370
at scale, like
continuous integration

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.220
and continuous deployment tools.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.980
So to start with, let's
create a deployment

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.950
like we did in the last
video, but this time we'll

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.509
do it declaratively.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.790
So here you can see that I'm
in Google Cloud platform,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.590
and I'm using the
Cloud Shell terminal

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.110
to interact with a
Kubernetes cluster

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.700
that I have already created.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.730
And if I run LS here,
you'll see that I'm

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:28.590
in a directory that has some
subdirectories and some files.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.890
So the files for my
microservice application

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.650
that I'm going to need to create
that microservice application

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.520
in Kubernetes are all
in these directories.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.730
So the first one that
I'm going to look at is

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.550
deployments/hello.yml.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.270
So in this case, I have
all of my files separated

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:52.195
by the type of Kubernetes
resource that they define.

00:02:52.195 --> 00:02:53.820
You could do this in
all sorts of ways,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.350
you don't have to do it with
this structure or anything.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.252
It's just the way
that it is here.

00:02:58.252 --> 00:02:59.460
So let's take a look at this.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.590
If you remember
the last video, we

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.590
had told Kubernetes what it was
that we wanted it to create,

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.370
which is a deployment
in this case.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.920
So we're doing that again here.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.660
We're giving that
deployment a name.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.980
We're telling Kubernetes
a number of replicas

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.660
that we want of the pods that
will be in this deployment.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.250
You remember that we
went over those as well.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.560
And so here, we are defining
the actual containers that we're

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.360
going to be running,
the container image,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.210
and a bit more
information about that.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.620
And you'll also
see down here, we

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.990
have a liveness probe
and a readiness probe,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.180
which we didn't really
go for last time.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.280
But they are tools that
Kubernetes provides

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.970
to help make sure that
your application is up

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.820
and ready to receive traffic.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.880
So there's a bit more that you
can learn about deployments.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.060
As you can see,
this is a fairly--

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.860
it's not really
that long, but there

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.650
are a few components in it that
are worth learning a bit more

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:56.170
about.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.860
So if you want to look into
deployments a bit more,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.940
I'd recommend it.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:06.990
So this is what we mean by
declaring our desired state.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.550
All of the
information about what

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.380
we want this deployment to
look like is here in this file.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:12.900
And if we wanted
to make a change,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.445
like changing the
image that we're

00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:17.070
going to be running
in this deployment,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.950
we could do that in this file
and we could track that change

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.470
through source control.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.380
So the next thing I'm
going to do, actually,

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:30.310
I want to look at one more
file before I create anything.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.600
So let me look at,
within services,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.480
I have another file
called Hello hello.yml.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.470
So if you remember
from the last video,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.070
we created our deployment
with our application

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.250
but then we needed some way
to reach our application.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.280
And we did that by creating
a Kubernetes service.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.190
So this is what
this is going to do.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.103
This is creating another
Kubernetes service

00:04:52.103 --> 00:04:53.520
that will allow
artists to connect

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.940
to our Hello application,
which will be

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.970
deployed in its own deployment.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.210
So the service definition
is pretty simple.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.970
It just has a name
for the service.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:08.480
It has a selecter which
tells the service how

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.210
to find the back ends
that it is serving,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.770
and then it has some
port information

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:18.320
that will allow us to actually
reach that application.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.500
So if I want to
create these and I

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:24.280
want to do it in a declarative
way, what I'm going to do

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:30.800
is run kubectl apply dash f
and give it the file name.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.600
So let's say
deployments/hello.yml.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.220
And I can do more
than one at a time.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:43.120
I can just add another dash f
here, and specify my service,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.000
and create my service
at the same time.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.230
So that's pretty cool.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.950
The kubectl apply command
doesn't know which

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.610
API object you're creating.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.950
So you can specify
it in the file

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.170
and then just give
it those files.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.410
So it's pretty versatile.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.120
So let's go ahead
and create those.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.700
So you can see that my
deployment and my service

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:04.840
were created.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.240
But it wouldn't be a
microservice application

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.660
if it wasn't split up
into multiple services.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.730
So the next service that
I'm going to deploy,

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.810
I'm going to deploy in
exactly the same way.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.880
I'm going to do
kubectly apply dash f,

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.610
and then grab
another deployment.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.870
And this time I'm going
to deploy my frontend.yml.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:30.010
So for this application I've
added in an Nginx server,

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:31.870
which will act as my front end.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.540
And by the way, if you want to
try this stuff out for yourself

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:38.467
and follow along, or just
kind of play around with it,

00:06:38.467 --> 00:06:39.550
check out the links below.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.750
We'll have some information for
you to get hands on with this.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.730
So we're going to
kubectl apply and we'll

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.100
give it a file for our
front end deployment.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:54.190
And once again, we're going
to need a service for that.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:59.855
So let's create our
frontend.yml service as well.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.700
Now, an interesting
thing here is

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.300
I'm using the
kubectl apply command

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.470
because, if that
resource doesn't exist,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.540
the apply command will go
ahead and create the resource.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:20.070
So I didn't have a front end
deployment or a front end

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.713
service before.

00:07:21.713 --> 00:07:23.130
So I went ahead
and created those.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:26.310
But if I were to run
this command again,

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.360
you'll see that it says that
the front end and the deployment

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.190
and its service were unchanged.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:37.380
So if we were to make a
change to our deployment,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.250
like I said earlier, if we were
to update the container image

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.957
or something like that
in our deployment file,

00:07:44.957 --> 00:07:47.040
where we have declared
what we want our deployment

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:51.090
to look like, then we would be
able to just apply that change.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.850
And in the case of
deployments, there

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.650
are settings within
Kubernetes that you

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:59.040
can use to tell it how to
roll out that change when

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.330
you apply a new change.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:05.040
So in that way, this is
a very declarative way

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.610
to create resources
in Kubernetes.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:18.567
But now that I have an Nginx
component to my application,

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:20.150
there's some
configuration information

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.610
that I want to pass
into my Nginx server.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.830
And it didn't really make
sense to add that stuff

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.640
into the container definition.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.440
And it didn't really
make sense to add

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:33.049
that at any point along the
way in creating the Kubernetes

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:34.130
deployment.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:36.710
So how am I going to get that
configuration information

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:39.320
into my Nginx server now?

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:43.940
Luckily, Kubernetes has a tool
for that called ConfigMaps.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:48.170
A config map lets you map
your configuration information

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:51.380
to your application running
in a Kubernetes deployment.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.650
I already have a
file here, which

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.110
has the configuration
information that I

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.180
need for my Nginx front end.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.583
And it's in nginx/frontedn.conf.

00:09:02.583 --> 00:09:04.250
So you can see here
that this is a bunch

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.940
of configuration information
for my Nginx front end.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.440
But you'll see that
it's not in yml form.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.020
So this is not actually
declaratively declared.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.780
So I'm going to have
to create this just

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:21.540
using the configuration
file as it is.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.340
So what I'm going to do
is run kubectl create.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:29.592
Configmap is the
type of Kubernetes

00:09:29.592 --> 00:09:30.800
object that I want to create.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:32.400
And I'm going to
give that a name,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.730
which I'm just going to call
it Nginx front end conf,

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:40.730
and I'm going to tell it I want
it to create that config map

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:48.140
from a file with
information file,

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.290
which is nginx/frontend.conf.

00:09:53.093 --> 00:09:55.260
Cool.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.710
So this, we'll go ahead
and create the config map

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.510
from that configuration
information

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.390
that I showed you a minute ago.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.880
But I have another problem.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.450
I want to run this application
a bit more securely

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:11.740
and I want to use TLS with it.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:13.490
But for that, I'm going
to need to pass it

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:16.760
some TLS certs,
which I don't really

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.700
want to pass in a config map
because that information is

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.190
just kind of plain text.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:26.150
I want to make sure that that
information is kept more, well,

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.710
secret.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:31.970
So Kubernetes has a great
tool for this called Secrets.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.980
Now, there are a lot
of different tools

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.140
for passing sensitive
information in Kubernetes.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.160
So Kubernetes Secrets
might not be the best tool

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.330
for your use case.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:44.520
You'll have to do a little bit
of research to figure it out.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.220
But in my case,
Kubernetes Secrets

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:47.480
are going to be just fine.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.160
They'll encrypt the
information that I'm

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:50.610
passing into my application.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:55.220
So let's go ahead and create a
secret to pass my application,

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.780
the TLS certs that
I have for it.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:01.220
So kubectl create is what
I'm going to use again.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.290
Secrets are very
similar to ConfigMaps,

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:06.590
except they're for
secret information.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:08.900
So create a secret.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.000
And there are a few
different types of secrets,

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:14.150
but for this one I'm just
going to create a generic one.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:17.750
And I'm going to give
it a name, tls-certs.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:21.590
And I'm going to create that,
once again, from a file.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:27.860
And that will be the TLS certs
that I have in my directory.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:30.620
So that secret was created.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:34.310
And now there's one more piece
of my microservice application.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:38.450
And that is an
authentication tool

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:41.180
that I've added into
the microservice.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:44.510
So what that's going to look
like is, once again, coming

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:49.160
back to that trusty
kubectl apply dash f,

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:55.160
and I'm going to grab a service
for my auth.yml component

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:56.600
of my micra service.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.390
And of course, I'm going to
need something for that service

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:02.480
to serve, which will be
a deployment one again.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.920
And that's going to
be auth.yml as well.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.447
And I appear to have
mistyped something.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:20.750
Oh, I didn't put a dash f
before my deployments file.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.623
So you do have to
put a dash f for each

00:12:22.623 --> 00:12:23.790
file that you're passing it.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:24.830
So there you go.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:26.450
You can see that
I've gone on ahead

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.400
and created the service
in the deployment

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:32.000
for my authentication
component of my microservice.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.470
And that's it.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.090
That's my whole
microservice that I'm

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:37.200
going to be showing you today.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.920
But how do I make sure
that it's working properly?

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.980
Well, the best way is
always to test, right?

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:47.090
So I'm going to run
kubectl get services,

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.790
or just svc for short.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.440
And I want to get the
information for my front end.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:54.710
So you'll see that
my front end service

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:56.780
is of type load balancer.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:00.170
In this case, that means that it
created an actual load balancer

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:05.600
resource in Google Cloud, and
that external load balancer

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:08.720
resource has created
an external IP for us

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:11.120
that we will actually
be able to use

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:14.850
to reach the application
that I've created.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.870
So I'm going to copy that IP
and I'm going to export it

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:19.940
as a variable just to
make my following commands

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:22.942
a little bit easier.

00:13:22.942 --> 00:13:24.220
EXTERNAL_IP.

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:30.420
And give it that number.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.140
OK.

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.130
So I've created my variable.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:36.060
So now I can run a
curl command to test

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:39.450
to make sure that my application
is running correctly.

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.240
And if you'll
remember earlier, we

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.670
incorporated some
security capabilities

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:46.920
into this application.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.990
But let's say that I
just curled that IP.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:52.740
What would happen?

00:13:55.525 --> 00:13:57.150
Actually, I'm just
going to post it in,

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.317
even though I just did that.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.960
So if I curl that,
nothing happens.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.490
Why is that?

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.180
Of course, it's
because we set up TLS

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.365
and we set up some
security measures.

00:14:11.365 --> 00:14:13.740
So now we're going to have to
connect to this application

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.420
using HTTPS.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:16.950
A bit more securely.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:26.220
So I'm going to run curl dash k
htttps:// and give it that IP.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:27.970
And there you go.

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:32.220
So we've got our
message output of hello.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.440
And that's all I expected
this application to do.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:37.470
So I know the application
is up and running properly.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:41.350
But I also incorporated this
authentication capability.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:43.860
And so I can also connect
to this application

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:48.790
as a specific user and
make use of it that way.

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.230
So for that, I'm going to
have to get a token to log in

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:53.590
as a specific user.

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:55.282
So what that's
going to look like--

00:14:55.282 --> 00:14:56.046
whoops.

00:14:56.046 --> 00:14:57.150
I already did it.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.100
So I'm going to
go ahead and enter

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.850
the password for the user.

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:03.570
So what you can see
here is I set up

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:09.390
a token, which equals
a curl command, logging

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.130
in as a specific user.

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:13.950
I'm grabbing the token
from that log in.

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:15.730
I had to enter the
password for that user.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.940
And so now I have this
token that I can use

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.290
to access the website as well.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:24.870
So now I'm going to go ahead
and run a curl command where

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.670
I am passing in that token.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:29.340
So I'm logging in
as a specific user.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:31.420
And if I run that, I
get the same result.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.950
So I know that I can
reach my application

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:40.020
by using this authorization
at capability that I edited.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:41.490
So cool stuff.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:46.890
I went through and I created
deployments and config maps

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:48.810
and secrets.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.780
And for the deployments
and the services,

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:55.650
I did it in a declarative
way, using these files

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:59.460
that I can then go in and
make changes to, track

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.820
those changes, and do a
lot of really cool stuff

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.770
with automation and running
applications at scale.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.095
So this is going to
be really useful.

00:16:08.095 --> 00:16:08.970
CARTER MORGAN: Great.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:12.055
So that's how I can run my
application declaratively.

00:16:12.055 --> 00:16:12.930
KASLIN FIELDS: Right.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.670
Running things
declaratively is really

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:16.260
useful in the real world.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.120
Running your deployments
based on a file

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.820
makes it easy to set up
CI/CD, source control,

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:23.550
and other processes that are
useful for running applications

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:24.648
at scale.

00:16:24.648 --> 00:16:25.440
CARTER MORGAN: Wow.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.940
Thanks to Kaslin
for taking the time

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.470
to show me how this all works.

